-- 0164_rebuild_catchup_columns_and_indexes.sql  (2026-09-18, schema-rebuild catch-up 3 of 5)
--
-- Part three of the migrations-can-rebuild-the-database catch-up. 0002_a and
-- 0002_b created the missing enum types and the 437 missing tables early in the
-- series, so that the ~150 historical ALTER/CREATE INDEX statements that
-- referenced them stopped failing. This file closes what is left:
--
--   * 143 columns that shared/schema*.ts declares on tables migrations/ already
--     creates, but that no migration file ever adds;
--   * 1009 indexes that shared/schema*.ts declares and the replayed migration
--     series does not produce.
--
-- Every statement here is verified to be a no-op against production: all 143
-- columns and all 1009 indexes are present in the committed structure-only prod
-- dump, docs/recovery/2026-07-03-db-migration-recovery/prod-schema-2026-07-03.sql.
-- Indexes production does NOT have are deliberately NOT in this file — see
-- 0166_rebuild_catchup_declared_uniques.sql.
--
-- It runs at the END of the series, not at 0002, because these tables are
-- created by migrations spread across the whole history; adding their columns
-- at 0002 would just move the failure earlier.
--
-- 5 of the columns are NOT NULL in shared/schema*.ts but are emitted NULLABLE
-- here: they carry no DEFAULT, and back-filling live rows is a data migration,
-- which this file deliberately is not.
--   * provider_background_checks.provider_id
--   * franchisees.franchisee_id
--   * franchisees.primary_contact
--   * franchisees.email
--   * franchisees.contract_start_date
-- Production already has all 5 (nullable or not), so nothing is lost there.
--
-- No DO $$ blocks and no CONCURRENTLY: the file stays on the runner's
-- transactional path, and every statement is independently idempotent.

